  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "For positive \\(x\\), the expression \\(x+4/x\\) can attain a minimum value. What is this minimum value?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "4",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "The product of \\(x\\) and \\(4/x\\) is 4, so the minimum value of their sum is \\(2\\sqrt{4}=4\\), attained when \\(x=2\\).\n\nAlternative method 1: Express the gap as a square\n\nSince \\(x>0\\), any square divided by \\(x\\) is nonnegative. Subtracting \\(4\\) exposes such a square:\n\\[\nx+\\frac4x-4\n=\\frac{x^2-4x+4}{x}\n=\\frac{(x-2)^2}{x}\\ge0.\n\\]\nEquality holds exactly at \\(x=2\\), which is allowed, so the minimum is \\(4\\).",
